# Public site: no speculative crawls (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-10-30): stop burning analoginfo.com
bandwidth on automated discovery. Webflow Technical Support (Marcel)
reported roughly 10 GB of 404 delivery in the reviewed period. The
site was probed for WordPress paths that do not exist
(`/wp-admin/`, `/wlwmanifest.xml`, assorted PHP filenames). Each
miss returned the full 404 page (about 236 KB uncompressed at that
time). 98% of page traffic scored as automated. The October 19 peak
was automated Python pulls plus scanner traffic, not a visitor event.

ChatGPT already retired `AnalogInformatics-SiteAudit/1.0` and changed
`submit-indexnow.py` so a no-argument run refuses the network. These
Cursor rules keep that posture.

## The rule

> **Request only exact known analoginfo.com URLs required by the
> current change and, when a blog release is in scope, the mandatory
> all-blog gate. No path guessing. No recursive crawl. No WordPress
> or PHP discovery. No scheduled full-site audit. Stop before an
> error body. IndexNow takes an explicit list of changed indexable
> production URLs. A no-argument or whole-sitemap IndexNow submit is
> forbidden.**

## Allowed live URLs

A live GET or HEAD is allowed only when all of these hold:

1. The URL is already known (handoff, sitemap row already on disk,
   CMS slug, or the page just published).
2. The current change requires that URL (or the all-blog gate does).
3. No verified local copy or receipt covers the check.
4. The request uses a named User-Agent from
   [`public-site-bandwidth-budget.mdc`](public-site-bandwidth-budget.mdc)
   and stays inside that budget.

Routine asset checks are HEAD. HTML metadata, link, sitemap, robots,
and JSON-LD checks are HTML-only. Do not launch a full browser for
those.

## Stop before error bodies

A 403, 404, 429, 5xx, unexpected content type, or redirect stops
dependent downloads. Do not fetch the error page to "see what it
looks like."

One bounded 404-size sample is allowed only when the 404 page or
shared global code that affects it changed. That sample uses the
budgeted client and stops after the documented cap. Inspect a
redirect destination before deliberately requesting it. No automatic
retry loops.

## IndexNow

After a **production** publish of analoginfo.com, run
`docs/go-to-market/website-and-ui/scripts/submit-indexnow.py` from
pam-platform-docs with the changed indexable URLs as arguments.

| Required | Forbidden |
| --- | --- |
| Explicit changed production URLs | No-argument run (script must refuse the network) |
| Cap 100 URLs per release | Whole-sitemap default |
| Skip `/search` | Submitting `/404`, `/wp-admin`, or probe paths |
| User-Agent `AnalogInformatics-IndexNow/2.0 (+https://analoginfo.com/contact)` | `AIC-indexnow` |
| Key location `https://analoginfo.com/{key}.txt` | `/.well-known/` as keyLocation |
| Deduplicate | Repeating an accepted Google live-test URL to check status |

Google does not use IndexNow. Keep `sitemap.xml` for Google. Do not
GET the live sitemap solely to feed IndexNow.

## Retired crawlers

Do not run, port, or "just once" revive:

- `AnalogInformatics-SiteAudit/1.0` and `audit_discovery.py`
- Any script that guesses `/wp-admin/`, `/wlwmanifest.xml`,
  `wp-login.php`, `xmlrpc.php`, or other PHP / WordPress paths
- Link-checker browser fleets
- Scheduled full-site audits
- Recursive feed or asset enumeration
- Python that bulk-GETs blog PNGs or competitor PDFs (the October 19
  pattern)

Historical validators stay retired until they use `bandwidth_http.py`
and these controls.

## Lean 404 (do not grow it)

The October 30 production 404 (page ID `6a92916292f4435c8aa7390c`)
removed Navbar, Footer, and Global Styles component instances. Measured
baseline: 105,694 raw HTML bytes and 30,304 encoded body bytes. Keep
raw HTML below 110,000 bytes. Do not add full navigation, footer, large
graphics, videos, forms, or marketing components. Keep the approved
title, explanation, compact recovery links, and the privacy-preferences
launcher. Recheck one bounded 404 sample only when that page or shared
global code changes.

## Forbidden

- Guessing paths to see whether they exist
- Reading 404 / 429 / 5xx bodies as a matter of course
- Sitemap-wide IndexNow or a no-argument submit
- Empty User-Agent discovery
- Concurrent crawlers
- Claiming scanner / WordPress-probe traffic is "not ours" and then
  adding our own probe to measure it

## Required agent behavior

1. Write the exact URL list before the first live request.
2. On any unexpected status, stop. Do not follow a guessed next path.
3. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same
   change set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).

## Origin

2026-10-30 - Webflow Support: about 10 GB of 404s from WordPress / PHP
probes at about 236 KB each, plus AIC-named User-Agents and Python
pulls. Strategy change: no speculative crawls; IndexNow explicit only.
